perf(landing): optimize the landing page against modern-web-guidance

Hand scroll effects, rendering cost and accessibility to native browser
features; JavaScript only fills in where support is missing.

Scroll and motion
- Move reading progress, nav shrink, reveals and parallax to scroll()/view()
  timelines. Timelines live in their own rules so the minifier cannot fold
  them into the `animation` shorthand, which Chrome then discards.
- Drive the progress bar with compositor `scale` instead of per-frame width;
  cache the scroll range with a ResizeObserver.
- Stagger grid cards with sibling-index(); use linear() spring easing.
- Let scroll-target-group + :target-current mark the current nav item; sync
  aria-current two frames later, since Chromium resolves :target-current one
  frame after scrollend.

Rendering cost
- Apply content-visibility: auto below the fold, with placeholder heights
  calibrated to measured content boxes (within 1px of real height at all
  three viewports, so scrolling no longer jumps).
- Gate the simulations on both IntersectionObserver and
  contentvisibilityautostatechange.

Accessibility and typography
- Make sections focusable; land anchors below the shrunk nav and keep the
  focus ring inside the region.
- Tie scrollbars, prefers-contrast, text-scale, text-wrap and font-size-adjust
  to the page palette.
- Fix .kiri p / .kiri h1 resets outranking component classes: use :where() to
  restore 17 paragraph margins plus heading weight and tracking that were
  silently dropped.
- Turn the share notice into a native details disclosure, animated with
  interpolate-size.
- Stop clipping the engine switches and path tabs at 320px.

Other
- Preload the faces the landing page actually paints with; docs keep Inter.
- Drop data-astro-prefetch attributes duplicating prefetchAll in astro.config.ts.
- Reserve the scrollbar lane with overflow-y: scroll instead of
  scrollbar-gutter: stable, which makes Chromium abort a view transition
  whenever a section holds focus.
